-- Q3891: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The general public and all potential investors","Only the service organization''s board of directors","Management, user entities, and prospective user entities with sufficient knowledge","Only the service auditor''s firm and its affiliates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AICPA SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), SOC 2 reports are restricted-use reports intended for management of the service organization, user entities, and prospective user entities with sufficient understanding to interpret the report. Unlike SOC 3 reports, they are not designed for general public distribution.
Wrong (A): SOC 2 reports are restricted-use and not intended for the general public or potential investors without a business relationship.
Wrong (B): The audience extends well beyond just the board of directors to include user entities and their auditors.
Wrong (D): The report is not limited to the service auditor''s firm; it serves multiple stakeholder groups with legitimate business needs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3891;

-- Q3895: SOC Reporting [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Using different methods for subservice organizations is not permitted in one report","The service auditor must issue two separate reports for each method used","The report must apply the carve-out method uniformly when any subservice uses it","The inclusive method brings one provider into scope while the other is carved out with CSOCs"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AT-C Section 320.14, a service organization may use different subservice organization presentation methods within the same SOC report. The inclusive method brings the network security provider''s controls into scope for direct testing, while the carve-out method excludes the data center operator and identifies its complementary subservice organization controls (CSOCs).
Wrong (A): AT-C 320 permits using different methods for different subservice organizations within one report based on engagement circumstances.
Wrong (B): A single SOC report can accommodate both methods without requiring separate reports.
Wrong (C): There is no requirement that the carve-out method must be applied uniformly when one subservice uses it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3895;

-- Q3896: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Marketing materials, customer testimonials, and competitive positioning statements","Engagement scope, management''s responsibilities, auditor''s responsibilities, criteria, and opinion","User entities'' financial statements and whether those statements are fairly presented","Employee handbook, organizational chart, and compensation structure details"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C Section 320.53, the independent service auditor''s report must include the engagement scope, management''s responsibilities for the system description and controls, the auditor''s responsibilities, the criteria used, and the auditor''s opinion on fairness and control effectiveness. These elements provide the structured foundation for reliance.
Wrong (A): Marketing materials and customer testimonials are entirely outside the engagement scope and have no place in the auditor''s report.
Wrong (C): User entities'' financial statements are not within the scope of a SOC 2 engagement, which focuses on the service organization''s system and controls.
Wrong (D): Employee handbooks and compensation structures are internal HR documents unrelated to the auditor''s report requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3896;

-- Q3897: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Exceptions are isolated, immaterial to trust services criteria, and not systemic control failures","Any exception automatically requires a qualified opinion regardless of materiality","Exceptions are removed from the report so it presents only effective controls","The report must be reissued after the organization remediates all exceptions found"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT-C 205, an unqualified opinion can be issued even when minor exceptions exist, provided the exceptions are not material to the trust services criteria and do not represent systemic control failures. The auditor evaluates whether exceptions are isolated incidents or indicate a pattern of control weakness.
Wrong (B): Materiality and systemic nature are the determining factors, not the mere existence of any exception.
Wrong (C): Exceptions are disclosed in the report with testing results; they are never removed to present a misleading picture of control effectiveness.
Wrong (D): The auditor issues the opinion based on the examination period as tested; there is no requirement to delay for remediation and retesting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3897;

-- Q3900: SOC Reporting [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The service auditor''s fee structure for the engagement","The total number of pages contained in the report","The service organization''s recent stock price performance","The service auditor''s competence and independence, scope, period, and exceptions noted"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C Section 402, before relying on a SOC 1 Type II report, the user entity''s auditor must evaluate the service auditor''s professional competence and independence, whether the report covers the relevant period, the scope of controls tested, and any reported exceptions. The auditor must also assess complementary user entity controls (CUECs).
Wrong (A): The service auditor''s fee has no bearing on the reliability or relevance of the report for audit purposes.
Wrong (B): The number of pages is irrelevant to the report''s sufficiency as audit evidence.
Wrong (C): Stock price performance is unrelated to evaluating whether a SOC report can be relied upon for audit evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3900;

-- Q3904: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Services unrelated to the engagement scope that do not affect evaluated criteria","The access control procedures for the in-scope application systems","The infrastructure components supporting the in-scope services delivered","The people and procedures involved in delivering the in-scope services"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AICPA SOC Guidance (DC Section 200) and AT-C Section 320.35, the system description must include all components relevant to in-scope services, while services outside the engagement scope that do not affect the criteria being evaluated may be appropriately excluded.
Wrong (B): Access controls for in-scope applications are directly relevant to the trust services criteria and must be included in the system description.
Wrong (C): Infrastructure supporting in-scope services is part of the system boundary and must be described.
Wrong (D): People and procedures involved in delivering in-scope services are required components of the system description.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3904;

-- Q3905: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both use the same terminology as established by the PCAOB standards","There is no real difference between the terms; they are interchangeable","SOC 1 uses criteria while SOC 2 uses control objectives instead","SOC 1 objectives are management-defined for ICFR; SOC 2 criteria are AICPA-predefined"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): In SOC 1 engagements, management defines control objectives describing what controls should achieve for user entities'' internal control over financial reporting. In SOC 2 engagements, the criteria are the predefined Trust Services Criteria (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy) established by the AICPA.
Wrong (A): The PCAOB does not establish SOC terminology; these are AICPA standards under SSAE 18.
Wrong (B): The terms reflect fundamentally different concepts appropriate to each engagement type and are not interchangeable.
Wrong (C): This reverses the actual terminology; SOC 1 uses control objectives and SOC 2 uses trust services criteria.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3905;

-- Q3907: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether controls operated effectively over a specified period of time","Whether the system description is fairly presented and controls suitably designed at a date","Whether the service organization''s financial statements are materially correct overall","Whether user entities have implemented all required complementary controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320.39 (SSAE No. 18), the Type I opinion addresses whether the system description fairly presents the system as designed and implemented as of the specified date, and whether controls are suitably designed to achieve the stated criteria. Type I is a point-in-time design assessment.
Wrong (A): Operating effectiveness over a period is addressed by a Type II report, not a Type I report.
Wrong (C): SOC 2 reports do not address the service organization''s financial statements; that is outside the engagement scope.
Wrong (D): The service auditor''s opinion does not cover whether user entities have implemented their complementary controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3907;

-- Q3908: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the exceptions'' significance, assess risk impact, and request a remediation plan","Accept the qualified report without taking any further action on it","Immediately terminate the contract and migrate to a different provider","Ignore the change since the user entity bears no responsibility for provider controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance and AT-C Section 320, the user entity should evaluate the specific exceptions underlying the qualified opinion, assess their potential impact on operations and risk, and request a remediation plan with timelines. Additional monitoring or compensating controls may be warranted.
Wrong (B): Accepting a qualified report without evaluation could leave the user entity exposed to unmitigated risks from the identified control deficiencies.
Wrong (C): Immediate termination is a disproportionate response; a structured assessment should precede any relationship decisions.
Wrong (D): User entities retain responsibility for understanding how service provider control deficiencies affect their own risk profile.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3908;

-- Q3911: IT General Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A physical lock restricting access to the data center facility door","Annual security awareness training sessions for all technical staff","A written policy statement posted on the company intranet site","Automated technical enforcement requiring documented approval before production changes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per COBIT 2019 BAI06 (Manage Changes) and AICPA TSC CC8.1, technical enforcement controls that prevent production changes without an approved change ticket provide stronger assurance than administrative policies alone. Automated enforcement blocks unauthorized changes at the system level regardless of urgency or role.
Wrong (A): A physical lock on the data center door does not prevent a DBA from making logical database changes remotely or through authorized physical access.
Wrong (B): Annual security awareness training is a preventive administrative control that individuals can deliberately bypass under pressure.
Wrong (C): A written policy statement is an administrative control that the DBA already violated; it does not technically prevent unauthorized changes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3911;

-- Q3912: IT General Controls [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It replaces the need for conducting physical inventory counts entirely","It centralizes asset records, configurations, and relationships supporting ITGC processes","It automatically detects and fixes configuration errors in production systems","It generates revenue by licensing the organization''s asset data to third parties"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT BAI10 (Manage Configuration) and ITIL Service Asset and Configuration Management, a CMDB provides a centralized repository of IT assets, their configurations, dependencies, and relationships. This supports change impact analysis, incident root cause identification, and access control scoping across managed assets.
Wrong (A): A CMDB supplements but does not replace physical inventory verification; both are needed for complete asset management.
Wrong (C): A CMDB is a record-keeping system that tracks configurations; it does not automatically remediate or fix configuration errors.
Wrong (D): A CMDB is an internal IT governance tool, not a revenue-generating product for licensing to external parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3912;

-- Q3913: IT Audit Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No documentation is required for any risk acceptance decisions made","Only an informal email from the IT manager mentioning the risk identified","A formal risk acceptance record signed by management with rationale and review date","A vendor proposal for the replacement system and its projected costs"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-39 Section 3.2 and COBIT APO12.04, risk acceptance decisions must be formally documented with a risk acceptance statement identifying the specific risk, rationale for acceptance, residual risk level, authorizing manager, and reassessment date. This demonstrates due diligence and supports audit accountability.
Wrong (A): Risk management frameworks require formal documentation of all risk treatment decisions, including acceptance.
Wrong (B): An informal email lacks the rigor, authorization signatures, and traceability required by governance frameworks for risk acceptance.
Wrong (D): A vendor replacement proposal addresses future remediation options but does not document the current risk acceptance decision.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3913;

-- Q3914: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The missed reconciliation is immaterial since 11 of 12 months were completed","The auditor should evaluate the deviation''s cause, impact, and whether compensating procedures exist","The auditor should automatically issue a qualified opinion for any deviation","The auditor should disregard the gap since the control operated for most of the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.35), a missed occurrence of a periodic control represents a deviation that the service auditor must evaluate for cause, impact, and materiality. The auditor assesses whether it was an isolated staffing issue or indicative of a systemic weakness, and whether compensating procedures were performed.
Wrong (A): The auditor cannot simply dismiss a deviation as immaterial based on a percentage alone without proper evaluation of cause and impact.
Wrong (C): A single deviation does not automatically require a qualified opinion; materiality and systemic nature must be assessed first.
Wrong (D): The auditor has a professional obligation to evaluate every deviation and cannot disregard missed control occurrences.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3914;

-- Q3915: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Test every transaction processed by the automated control during the period","No testing is required for any automated controls in the system","Test a random sample of 60 items selected throughout the examination period","Test at one point in time and confirm through change management that code was unchanged"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320.A40 (SSAE No. 18), automated controls operate consistently as long as the underlying code remains unchanged. The auditor can test at a single point in time and verify through IT general controls that no modifications were made during the examination period.
Wrong (A): Testing every transaction is unnecessarily extensive for a consistently operating automated control with effective ITGCs.
Wrong (B): Automated controls still require testing; the auditor must verify the control functions as designed and that the code has not changed.
Wrong (C): Sample-based testing of 60 items applies to manual controls; automated controls with unchanged code need only single-point testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3915;

-- Q3919: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Express an opinion on the other information section''s content and accuracy","Read it for material inconsistencies with the system description without opining","Remove the other information section before the report is officially issued","Ignore the other information section entirely during the engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320, the service auditor reads the other information section to identify material inconsistencies with the audited system description or apparent material misstatements of fact, but does not express an opinion on it. If inconsistencies are found, the auditor discusses them with management.
Wrong (A): The auditor''s opinion covers only the system description and controls, not the other information section.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to remove management-provided other information from the report.
Wrong (D): The auditor has a professional responsibility to read the section even though no opinion is issued on it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3919;

-- Q4919: Change Management [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The control is operating effectively because 92.5% compliance exceeds the threshold","The deviation should be reported and evaluated for whether it represents a control deficiency","The finding is immaterial because only three changes were directly affected","The entire change management process should be considered completely ineffective"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 265 and COBIT BAI06, control deviations in change management must be reported and evaluated to determine whether they represent a deficiency, significant deficiency, or material weakness. The auditor assesses the root cause, frequency, and potential impact rather than applying arbitrary compliance thresholds.
Wrong (A): An arbitrary 90% compliance threshold is not an accepted standard; deviations must be evaluated based on cause, nature, and potential impact on financial reporting.
Wrong (C): The number of affected changes alone does not determine materiality; the auditor must assess the nature and potential consequences of the deviation.
Wrong (D): A single deviation does not automatically render the entire change management process ineffective; systemic versus isolated analysis is required.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4919;

-- Q4921: Change Management [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inability to identify recurring problems, measure effectiveness, and improve the process","Increased costs for hardware procurement and replacement cycles","Failure to submit change requests on time for scheduled maintenance windows","Loss of CMDB backup data and related configuration records"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT BAI06 and ITIL Change Management, without post-implementation reviews, an organization cannot identify recurring problems, measure whether changes achieved their intended outcomes, or drive continuous improvement. This represents the primary risk of omitting this critical feedback loop.
Wrong (B): Hardware procurement costs are not directly affected by the absence of post-implementation reviews in change management.
Wrong (C): Timeliness of change request submissions is a process compliance issue unrelated to post-implementation review practices.
Wrong (D): CMDB backup data loss is an IT operations concern unrelated to whether post-implementation reviews are conducted.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4921;

-- Q4922: Change Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Change management is an ITGC category ensuring modifications are authorized, tested, and documented","Change management replaces the need for all other IT general controls entirely","IT general controls are a subset of change management procedures overall","Change management only applies to application-level changes, not infrastructure changes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT BAI06 and AU-C 315, change management is one of several IT general control categories (alongside access controls, operations, and program development) that ensures modifications to systems and applications are properly authorized, tested, and documented before implementation.
Wrong (B): Change management is one component of ITGCs alongside access controls, operations, and program development; it does not replace them.
Wrong (C): The relationship is reversed; change management is a subset of the broader ITGC framework, not the other way around.
Wrong (D): Change management applies to all IT changes including infrastructure, databases, operating systems, and network configurations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4922;

-- Q4923: Change Management [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It eliminates the need for rollback procedures during the deployment","It allows the organization to halt, fix the issue, and protect remaining business units","It guarantees that no data will be lost during migration between systems","It reduces the total cost of the deployment compared to a big-bang approach"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT BAI06 (Manage Changes) and ITIL Release Management, a phased rollout limits the blast radius of deployment issues. When errors are discovered in an early phase, the organization can halt the rollout, resolve the problem, and prevent the same issue from affecting remaining business units.
Wrong (A): Phased rollouts do not eliminate the need for rollback procedures; rollback plans remain essential for each phase in case issues arise.
Wrong (C): A phased approach reduces risk but does not guarantee zero data loss; data migration issues can still occur within each phase.
Wrong (D): Phased rollouts may actually increase total cost due to extended timelines and repeated deployment activities across phases.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4923;

-- Q4935: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To calculate the total cost of data storage infrastructure requirements","To benchmark the platform''s performance against competing solutions","To identify, assess, and mitigate privacy risks to individuals before processing begins","To determine the number of database administrators needed for operations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GDPR Article 35, a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) is required for high-risk processing activities. Its primary objective is to systematically identify, assess, and mitigate privacy risks to data subjects before processing begins, evaluating necessity, proportionality, and compliance measures.
Wrong (A): A DPIA focuses on privacy risks to individuals, not on calculating infrastructure or storage costs.
Wrong (B): Competitive benchmarking is a business strategy activity unrelated to the privacy risk assessment purpose of a DPIA.
Wrong (D): Staffing decisions for database administrators are operational matters outside the scope of a DPIA.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4935;

-- Q4939: Data Protection Technologies [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["AES-256 is not a strong enough encryption algorithm for backup data","Off-site storage facilities cannot properly handle tapes with encryption","Backup tapes are technically incompatible with AES-256 encryption methods","An attacker who obtains the tapes has both the ciphertext and decryption keys"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-111 (Guide to Storage Encryption Technologies), encryption keys must be stored separately from the encrypted data they protect. Storing keys on the same tapes means an attacker who obtains the tapes has everything needed to decrypt the data, completely negating the encryption protection.
Wrong (A): AES-256 is widely considered one of the strongest symmetric encryption algorithms available and is fully suitable for backup encryption.
Wrong (B): Off-site storage facilities routinely handle encrypted tapes; encryption is a standard practice for off-site backup security.
Wrong (C): Backup tape systems fully support AES-256 encryption; many enterprise tape drives include hardware encryption capabilities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4939;

-- Q4948: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["SOC 1 addresses ICFR controls while SOC 2 addresses trust services criteria","SOC 1 reports are always Type I while SOC 2 reports are always Type II","SOC 1 reports are public documents while SOC 2 reports are restricted-use","SOC 2 reports only apply to cloud service providers and hosting companies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): SOC 1 reports (under SSAE 18 / AT-C 320) evaluate controls relevant to user entities'' internal control over financial reporting (ICFR). SOC 2 reports (under AT-C 205) evaluate controls based on the AICPA Trust Services Criteria covering security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy.
Wrong (B): Both SOC 1 and SOC 2 can be issued as either Type I (point-in-time) or Type II (period of time) reports.
Wrong (C): Both SOC 1 and SOC 2 reports are restricted-use; only SOC 3 reports are general-use public documents.
Wrong (D): SOC 2 reports apply to any service organization, not just cloud providers; they cover any entity providing services where trust services criteria are relevant.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4948;

-- Q4950: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A requirement to corroborate every management assertion with external evidence","An assumption that management is dishonest until proven otherwise by evidence","A refusal to accept any representations from management during the engagement","A questioning mind, alertness to misstatement indicators, and critical evidence assessment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 200.14, professional skepticism is an attitude that includes a questioning mind, alertness to conditions that may indicate possible misstatement due to fraud or error, and a critical assessment of audit evidence. It requires maintaining objectivity without assuming dishonesty.
Wrong (A): Professional skepticism does not require external corroboration for every assertion; it requires a questioning approach to evaluating all evidence.
Wrong (B): Skepticism presumes neither honesty nor dishonesty; it maintains a neutral, questioning stance toward management representations.
Wrong (C): The auditor can accept management representations as one form of evidence while still exercising professional skepticism.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4950;

-- Q4951: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The subservice organization''s controls are included in the SOC report scope for testing","The subservice organization is no longer responsible for any controls whatsoever","The subservice''s controls are excluded from scope and the report identifies its functions","The service auditor tests the subservice organization''s controls directly during the exam"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 320, the carve-out method excludes the subservice organization''s controls from the service organization''s SOC report scope. The report identifies the nature of services provided by the subservice organization and the functions it performs, but does not include testing of its controls.
Wrong (A): This describes the inclusive method, not the carve-out method; under carve-out, controls are excluded from scope.
Wrong (B): The subservice organization retains full responsibility for its controls; the carve-out method only affects reporting scope, not responsibility.
Wrong (D): Under the carve-out method, the service auditor does not test the subservice organization''s controls; user entities must obtain a separate SOC report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4951;

-- Q4954: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["SOC 3 reports are restricted to user entities while SOC 2 reports are publicly available","Both report types have identical distribution restrictions under AICPA standards","SOC 2 reports are restricted-use while SOC 3 reports are general-use and publicly available","SOC 3 reports are only available to regulatory agencies and government bodies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 320 and AICPA guidance, SOC 2 reports are restricted-use reports available only to user entities, their auditors, and others with sufficient knowledge. SOC 3 reports are general-use reports that can be freely distributed, including posting on the company''s website for public access.
Wrong (A): This reverses the actual distribution rules; SOC 2 is restricted and SOC 3 is publicly available, not the opposite.
Wrong (B): The two report types have fundamentally different distribution models; SOC 2 is restricted while SOC 3 is general-use.
Wrong (D): SOC 3 reports are available to the general public, not limited to regulatory agencies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4954;

-- Q4955: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The bridge period, which may require a bridge letter from the service organization","The testing window for evaluating control operating effectiveness","The remediation period for addressing identified control deficiencies","The pre-engagement phase before the audit fieldwork officially begins"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C Section 402, the gap between a SOC report''s coverage period end and the user entity''s fiscal year-end is known as the bridge period (or gap period). The user entity''s auditor may require a bridge letter from the service organization confirming no significant changes to controls during this gap.
Wrong (B): The testing window refers to the SOC report''s examination period, not the gap after it ends.
Wrong (C): The remediation period is not a standard term for this gap; remediation refers to correcting identified deficiencies.
Wrong (D): The pre-engagement phase precedes the audit engagement and occurs before the SOC report period, not after it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4955;

-- Q4956: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The description contains no information whatsoever about any system weaknesses","The description includes every technical detail about the organization''s infrastructure","The description has been reviewed and approved by the organization''s external legal counsel","The description meets criteria and does not omit or distort relevant information"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AT-C Section 320 and SSAE 18, "fairly presents" means the system description is presented in accordance with the description criteria and does not omit or distort information relevant to the scope of the engagement. It requires accuracy and completeness within scope, not exhaustive technical detail.
Wrong (A): Fairly presenting does not mean hiding weaknesses; relevant control deficiencies within scope must be disclosed.
Wrong (B): Fairly presents requires relevant and material information, not every technical detail about the organization''s entire infrastructure.
Wrong (C): External legal counsel review is not part of the "fairly presents" standard; this is a management responsibility under AICPA criteria.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4956;

-- Q4957: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether controls were operating effectively throughout a specified examination period","Whether user entities have implemented all of their complementary controls","Whether the service organization fully complies with GDPR regulatory requirements","Whether the description is fairly presented and controls suitably designed at a date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AT-C Section 320.39, the SOC 2 Type I opinion addresses whether the system description fairly presents the system as designed and implemented as of the specified date, and whether the controls are suitably designed to meet the applicable trust services criteria at that point in time.
Wrong (A): Operating effectiveness over a period is addressed by a Type II report, not a Type I report which is a point-in-time assessment.
Wrong (B): The service auditor''s opinion does not cover user entities'' implementation of their own complementary controls.
Wrong (C): GDPR compliance is not within the scope of the SOC 2 engagement opinion, which addresses trust services criteria.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4957;

-- Q4959: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Controls the user entity is expected to implement at their own sites","Controls required by the criteria but not implemented by any party involved","Controls that the service auditor implements during the testing procedures","Controls the service organization expects the subservice organization has implemented"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AT-C Section 320, complementary subservice organization controls (CSOCs) are controls that the service organization assumes the subservice organization has implemented for the overall control objectives to be achieved. These are identified in the system description when the carve-out method is used.
Wrong (A): Controls the user entity is expected to implement are called complementary user entity controls (CUECs), not CSOCs.
Wrong (B): Controls required but not implemented by any party would represent a control gap or deficiency, not a defined category.
Wrong (C): The service auditor does not implement controls; they test and evaluate controls that management has designed and implemented.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4959;

-- Q4960: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The report should have been split into two separate reports at the migration date","Data center changes are not within SOC 2 scope and can be disregarded entirely","The auditor should evaluate whether controls were tested before and after the migration","The migration automatically invalidates the service auditor''s opinion on controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 320 and SSAE 18, when a material system change such as a data center migration occurs during the examination period, the auditor should evaluate whether controls were tested in both the pre-migration and post-migration environments. This ensures the opinion reflects control effectiveness across the entire period.
Wrong (A): A mid-period system change does not require splitting the report; one report can cover the full period with appropriate testing of both environments.
Wrong (B): Data center migrations directly affect security and availability controls and are squarely within SOC 2 scope.
Wrong (D): A migration does not automatically invalidate the opinion; the auditor evaluates whether controls remained effective throughout the transition.$EXPL$
